Narrow syscall arguments (BOOLEAN, WORD, BYTE, ...) arrive from the
x86_64 syscall dispatcher with the undefined upper bits the Windows caller
left in the register or stack slot.  Clang compiles these SysV functions
assuming the caller zero/sign-extended them to 32 bits (GCC does not), so a
FALSE could test as TRUE (NtQueryDirectoryObject restart=TRUE made
GetLogicalDrives() loop forever).  SYSCALL_NARROW_ARG() (wine/unixlib.h)
makes them opaque at function entry.  The affected syscalls were listed by
scripts/debug/narrow-syscall-args.py in the openbsd-proton project.

Index: dlls/win32u/palette.c
